WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:03.603
-Mark Ronson in the house!
Welcome, buddy.

00:00:03.603 --> 00:00:05.071
I always --
-Congrats.

00:00:05.071 --> 00:00:08.107
-Oh. I guess it's no big deal.

00:00:08.107 --> 00:00:11.111
It's the first annual -- Yeah.

00:00:11.111 --> 00:00:13.312
It's -- Yeah,
it's kind of a shock to me.

00:00:13.312 --> 00:00:14.814
-May I touch it?
-No.

00:00:14.814 --> 00:00:16.549
I'd rather you not.
-Okay.

00:00:16.549 --> 00:00:18.985
-How are you doing, bud?
-I'm feel good.

00:00:18.985 --> 00:00:20.887
-Isn't that amazing
to come out to an audience?

00:00:20.887 --> 00:00:22.889
-This is so amazing.
It's incredible.

00:00:22.889 --> 00:00:24.324
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:24.324 --> 00:00:27.594
And you look so good.
-Oh, stop it.

00:00:27.594 --> 00:00:30.530
I've known you
for the longest time.

00:00:30.530 --> 00:00:32.265
-Yeah.
-I -- I just saw in the papers

00:00:32.265 --> 00:00:34.634
that you deejayed some event.

00:00:34.634 --> 00:00:36.369
Maybe it was a couple weeks ago.
It was a Gucci thing.

00:00:36.369 --> 00:00:39.405
It was this giant thing.
It was your first time out.

00:00:39.405 --> 00:00:41.507
How did it feel to be deejaying
in front of people again?

00:00:41.507 --> 00:00:43.008
It's been a year --
almost two years.

00:00:43.008 --> 00:00:44.577
-Yeah, it's crazy,
'cause it's something

00:00:44.577 --> 00:00:46.913
I've done my entire life
since I was 18.

00:00:46.913 --> 00:00:51.183
And I googled, and it said
I hadn't deejayed for 510 days.

00:00:51.183 --> 00:00:52.719
And I was terrified.

00:00:52.719 --> 00:00:55.555
And then I got out,
and I was just like, you know --

00:00:55.555 --> 00:00:58.691
After three songs of nerves,
it was great, and I loved it.

00:00:58.691 --> 00:01:01.894
-If all things fail, you can
always get up and just perform

00:01:01.894 --> 00:01:03.962
because who knew
that you were in a band --

00:01:03.962 --> 00:01:07.367
I did not know this --
called The Whole Earth Mamas.

00:01:07.367 --> 00:01:08.834
-Yeah.

00:01:08.834 --> 00:01:10.603
-Yeah, what happened
to these guys?

00:01:10.603 --> 00:01:11.804
-Whoa! [ Laughs ]

00:01:11.804 --> 00:01:13.573
-Quest, did you know this?
-No!

00:01:13.573 --> 00:01:16.075
-What is -- Mark,
I love that you save --

00:01:16.075 --> 00:01:17.877
-That's me.
-Oh, yeah, I know.

00:01:17.877 --> 00:01:20.713
What was --
what was Whole Earth Mamas?

00:01:20.713 --> 00:01:21.848
What was their vibe?

00:01:21.848 --> 00:01:23.783
-Okay, Whole Earth Mamas
was a band.

00:01:23.783 --> 00:01:25.819
It was my high-school band.

00:01:25.819 --> 00:01:27.587
I didn't particularly
like the name,

00:01:27.587 --> 00:01:28.921
but we were arguing over it

00:01:28.921 --> 00:01:30.823
before our first gig,
and our lead singer was like,

00:01:30.823 --> 00:01:32.625
"We're gonna get called
The Whole Earth Mamas."

00:01:32.625 --> 00:01:35.027
I was like, "No, we're not.
That's a terrible name."

00:01:35.027 --> 00:01:37.096
And then he had the mic,
and before our first song,

00:01:37.096 --> 00:01:38.764
he was like,
"Hey, ladies and gentlemen,

00:01:38.764 --> 00:01:39.866
we're The Whole Earth Mamas."

00:01:39.866 --> 00:01:41.267
And we just looked
at each other like,

00:01:41.267 --> 00:01:42.835
"I guess we're
The Whole Earth Mamas now."

00:01:42.835 --> 00:01:44.403
-What does it mean?
What's the meaning behind --

00:01:44.403 --> 00:01:46.705
-I --I think it was --
At the time,

00:01:46.705 --> 00:01:50.877
it was, like, a vegan, hippie
mail-order catalogue.

00:01:50.877 --> 00:01:52.278
And I don't know.
Like, seriously.

00:01:52.278 --> 00:01:53.979
-That does sound like
a good name for that.

00:01:53.979 --> 00:01:55.948
-Yeah, before there was,
like, Whole Foods

00:01:55.948 --> 00:01:58.284
and all this trendy stuff,
there was, like, this one --

00:01:58.284 --> 00:02:00.219
And I think his mom read it,

00:02:00.219 --> 00:02:02.221
and we were
The Whole Earth Mamas,

00:02:02.221 --> 00:02:05.024
and we got booked
for a lot of all-female,

00:02:05.024 --> 00:02:06.826
like, lineups and festivals

00:02:06.826 --> 00:02:08.761
because people thought
The Whole Earth Mamas --

00:02:08.761 --> 00:02:10.997
I wonder why --
were a group of women.

00:02:10.997 --> 00:02:12.364
So...

00:02:12.364 --> 00:02:13.900
-You would get booked,
and then you'd show up,

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:16.102
and they're like, "What are
these dudes doing here?"

00:02:16.102 --> 00:02:18.971
-Yeah, like, these kind of,
like, super, like amazing,

00:02:18.971 --> 00:02:21.641
like, all-feminist band lineups,
and we just show up.

00:02:21.641 --> 00:02:23.509
We were like 15,
so no one was gonna, like,

00:02:23.509 --> 00:02:24.744
throw crap at us.

00:02:24.744 --> 00:02:26.679
-I want to talk about --
You do so much.

00:02:26.679 --> 00:02:28.747
The podcast.
"The FADER Uncovered."

00:02:28.747 --> 00:02:31.450
-Yes.
-This is -- You --

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:35.121
One-on-one conversation
with amazing musicians

00:02:35.121 --> 00:02:37.257
who happen to be
on the cover of "FADER."

00:02:37.257 --> 00:02:38.691
-Yes.

00:02:38.691 --> 00:02:40.793
-Magazine. But how did you
get a chance to do this?

00:02:40.793 --> 00:02:43.363
-Well, the "FADER" came to me.
I love the "FADER" magazine.

00:02:43.363 --> 00:02:45.030
They always have really cool
people on the cover.

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:47.366
And they said, "Do you want
to do a thing where you talk

00:02:47.366 --> 00:02:49.536
to David Byrne,
Questlove, Erykah Badu?

00:02:49.536 --> 00:02:51.871
All of these great people
that have been on

00:02:51.871 --> 00:02:53.005
the cover of our magazine."

00:02:53.005 --> 00:02:54.307
So, I said, "Of course."

00:02:54.307 --> 00:02:56.542
I mean, I make music,
but I'm also --

00:02:56.542 --> 00:02:58.645
I think I'm more
of a fan of music

00:02:58.645 --> 00:03:00.079
than somebody who makes it.

00:03:00.079 --> 00:03:02.815
So the idea of talking and being
a nerd and asking David Byrne

00:03:02.815 --> 00:03:05.250
about The Talking Heads
and American Utopia

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:07.720
and all these things,
it's so exciting to me.

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:11.290
-I saw a trailer for "Watch
the Sound with Mark Ronson,"

00:03:11.290 --> 00:03:13.225
and you were talking
to Dave Grohl

00:03:13.225 --> 00:03:15.695
and you were talking to --
talking to Paul McCartney.

00:03:15.695 --> 00:03:17.063
-Yes.
-Which, I mean --

00:03:17.063 --> 00:03:19.131
I've known you forever.
I was like --

00:03:19.131 --> 00:03:21.167
-I know.
-Do you get nervous?

00:03:21.167 --> 00:03:23.369
-Of course.
I mean, everybody --

00:03:23.369 --> 00:03:24.837
You must even still get nervous.

00:03:24.837 --> 00:03:27.039
I mean, you've done so many
great sketches with him

00:03:27.039 --> 00:03:28.374
and all that stuff,
but you still get nervous.

00:03:28.374 --> 00:03:30.008
-Of course,
I'm nervous right now.

00:03:30.008 --> 00:03:31.677
-It's Paul McCartney.

00:03:31.677 --> 00:03:32.745
-But, yeah,
it's Paul McCartney.

00:03:32.745 --> 00:03:33.946
-No, I mean with
Paul McCartney, not me.

00:03:33.946 --> 00:03:35.314
-Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.
No. Yeah.

00:03:35.314 --> 00:03:36.783
I'm just always nervous.

00:03:36.783 --> 00:03:39.151
But, no, with Paul, I mean,
you get in there.

00:03:39.151 --> 00:03:41.187
This is like you're talking
about sampling.

00:03:41.187 --> 00:03:42.655
You're talking about
double-tracking.

00:03:42.655 --> 00:03:44.056
-Yeah.
-You're talking about things

00:03:44.056 --> 00:03:46.926
that we kind of even take
for granted when we hear songs.

00:03:46.926 --> 00:03:48.561
And what goes on
in the background

00:03:48.561 --> 00:03:49.995
that's so interesting.
-Exactly.

00:03:49.995 --> 00:03:52.899
This show is about the things
and the sounds

00:03:52.899 --> 00:03:55.267
and the tools
that, like, revolutionize music.

00:03:55.267 --> 00:03:57.436
So, you know,
you think of a song

00:03:57.436 --> 00:03:59.138
like "Pony" by Ginuwine.

00:03:59.138 --> 00:04:01.507
It's a great song if you sat
with an acoustic guitar,

00:04:01.507 --> 00:04:03.108
and you went,
"I'm just a bachelor."

00:04:03.108 --> 00:04:04.843
Like, it would probably
be a good song.

00:04:04.843 --> 00:04:06.579
But when you think of "Pony",
you think of...

00:04:06.579 --> 00:04:09.648
♪ Bwah! Bwah-bwah-bwah! ♪

00:04:09.648 --> 00:04:12.051
You know what I mean? Like,
that's when sound is great.

00:04:12.051 --> 00:04:13.419
Exactly.
-It's the greatest.

00:04:13.419 --> 00:04:15.021
-You do it.
You're better than me.

00:04:15.021 --> 00:04:18.124
-No, I'm not better than you.
We're equal.

00:04:18.124 --> 00:04:20.860
And, uh -- It's just that --

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:22.562
It's not a big deal.
They mean nothing.

00:04:22.562 --> 00:04:24.197
They mean nothing.

00:04:24.197 --> 00:04:25.764
No, but what --
did Paul -- did Paul

00:04:25.764 --> 00:04:27.566
like the finished product?

00:04:27.566 --> 00:04:29.769
-Well, yes.
So, anyway -- Yes.

00:04:29.769 --> 00:04:32.971
So, as the show was, like,
finishing up, I was --

00:04:32.971 --> 00:04:34.873
I knew that Paul
was going to see his scenes

00:04:34.873 --> 00:04:36.209
to see if he was happy with it.

00:04:36.209 --> 00:04:37.844
'Cause he's Paul McCartney.
He gets to be like,

00:04:37.844 --> 00:04:39.879
"Hey, you better not make me
look like an idiot."

00:04:39.879 --> 00:04:43.015
-It's tough to do, yeah.
-Yeah.

00:04:43.015 --> 00:04:45.918
So, I'm sitting at home
one day, and I look --

00:04:45.918 --> 00:04:48.955
and my phone vibrates,
and I look and it says --

00:04:48.955 --> 00:04:51.390
Like, whatever dumb thing
I have his number saved as

00:04:51.390 --> 00:04:53.292
in my phone, like,
PMC3000 or something.

00:04:53.292 --> 00:04:54.794
-Yeah.
-And I like, "What?"

00:04:54.794 --> 00:04:56.162
Like, he's never called me.

00:04:56.162 --> 00:04:58.498
I shouldn't even really
have his number.

00:04:58.498 --> 00:05:00.232
So I -- And it's a FaceTime.

00:05:00.232 --> 00:05:01.901
And it's 9:00 a.m. It's crazy.

00:05:01.901 --> 00:05:05.271
So, I answer, and he's like...
[as McCartney] "Hey, Mark."

00:05:05.271 --> 00:05:06.706
Like, you know --
You can do the thing.

00:05:06.706 --> 00:05:08.240
-[as McCartney]
"Hey, Mark. Good morning.

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:09.675
What are you doing? Yeah."
-Yeah, yeah.

00:05:09.675 --> 00:05:11.043
-And he just looks cool, right?

00:05:11.043 --> 00:05:12.878
-He looks like a golden god.
-Yeah, of course.

00:05:12.878 --> 00:05:14.346
-His, like, hair
is blowing around.

00:05:14.346 --> 00:05:16.215
And I'm just like, "Oh, God,
he's going to call

00:05:16.215 --> 00:05:17.916
and tell me that, like,
he hates the show.

00:05:17.916 --> 00:05:19.385
We're going to have to
edit it out.

00:05:19.385 --> 00:05:21.387
The show is ruined."
Like, you know, whatever.

00:05:21.387 --> 00:05:23.156
And he was like,
"So, I saw the show."

00:05:23.156 --> 00:05:25.324
And I'm like, yeah, wait for it.
This is going to be terrible.

00:05:25.324 --> 00:05:27.126
And he's like,
"It's pretty cool."

00:05:27.126 --> 00:05:28.728
I was like,
"Ah! Maybe it's not going to

00:05:28.728 --> 00:05:30.396
be, like, as bad as I think."
He was like,

00:05:30.396 --> 00:05:32.798
"Yeah, I really love what you
said about me and sampling

00:05:32.798 --> 00:05:35.401
and how you linked me to
Public Enemy and this and that."

00:05:35.401 --> 00:05:37.369
And I was like -- Wow.
-He watched it.

00:05:37.369 --> 00:05:40.740
-Yeah, and it was just cool
that he dug it.

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:42.909
And then he was like -- We
stopped talking about the show.

00:05:42.909 --> 00:05:45.010
And he was like,
"So what are you doing today?"

00:05:45.010 --> 00:05:47.379
And I was like, "Whoa."
And now I'm just shooting the --

00:05:47.379 --> 00:05:48.714
-Yeah.
-With Paul McCartney.

00:05:48.714 --> 00:05:50.182
-Wow!
-And then --

00:05:50.182 --> 00:05:52.718
-What'd you talk about?
-Well, I said,

00:05:52.718 --> 00:05:55.788
"I'm going to the studio."
I didn't know what to say.

00:05:55.788 --> 00:05:57.690
So he was like
"How's that going?"

00:05:57.690 --> 00:06:00.926
I'm like, "You know, I'm kind of
in love and I'm happy.

00:06:00.926 --> 00:06:02.995
So I've never written music
like that,

00:06:02.995 --> 00:06:04.730
you know, when you're happy."

00:06:04.730 --> 00:06:06.632
And he's like, "Why don't you
write that. then?"

00:06:06.632 --> 00:06:08.067
You know?
And I was like, "What?"

00:06:08.067 --> 00:06:09.669
He's like,
"I'm in love and I'm happy."

00:06:09.669 --> 00:06:11.337
So I'm, like,
scribbling the notes.

00:06:11.337 --> 00:06:13.238
Yeah, Paul McCartney
said write this song.

00:06:13.238 --> 00:06:16.041
-Paul McCartney wrote --
-"Any other lines?" You know?

00:06:16.041 --> 00:06:17.643
-Yeah, exactly.

00:06:17.643 --> 00:06:20.713
-And so I -- so I went,
and I jumped in the shower.

00:06:20.713 --> 00:06:23.281
I hope I don't get in trouble
for telling this story.

00:06:23.281 --> 00:06:25.217
And I look at my phone,
and there's, like, a little

00:06:25.217 --> 00:06:28.754
voice recording on my phone
from Paul McCartney.

00:06:28.754 --> 00:06:31.690
And he sung a little bit
of this song to tell me

00:06:31.690 --> 00:06:34.527
to go make the song --
"I'm in love and I'm happy."

00:06:34.527 --> 00:06:38.164
-Oh, my gosh! Dude, that is the
greatest story I've ever heard.

00:06:38.164 --> 00:06:39.532
That's unbelievable!
-It's crazy.

00:06:39.532 --> 00:06:43.135
So, I'm dropping it
on Spotify Friday.

00:06:43.135 --> 00:06:46.105
I mean, Apple Music.
-That is -- Yeah, exactly.

00:06:46.105 --> 00:06:48.974
I want to show a clip.
Everyone should check this out.

00:06:48.974 --> 00:06:52.511
Here is Mark Ronson and Paul
McCartney in "Watch the Sound."

00:06:52.511 --> 00:06:54.212
Take a look.

00:06:54.212 --> 00:06:56.949
-When you went into the studio,
the early Beatles stuff,

00:06:56.949 --> 00:06:59.252
you're just there to be
the best band you can be.

00:06:59.252 --> 00:07:01.019
And then when you start
to discover

00:07:01.019 --> 00:07:02.755
making the studio an instrument

00:07:02.755 --> 00:07:04.990
and then become as involved
with the tech stuff

00:07:04.990 --> 00:07:08.561
as you were with the songwriting
and playing of the instruments.

00:07:08.561 --> 00:07:10.563
-Yeah.

00:07:10.563 --> 00:07:15.467
When we arrived at Abbey Road,
it was post-war.

00:07:15.467 --> 00:07:19.538
So the engineers
would have lab coats.

00:07:19.538 --> 00:07:21.340
We were kids.

00:07:21.340 --> 00:07:23.108
We couldn't even come
in the main entrance.

00:07:23.108 --> 00:07:24.276
-Really?

00:07:24.276 --> 00:07:26.211
-We came in the
tradesman's entrance.

00:07:26.211 --> 00:07:29.181
-To hear all those stories?
-Yeah.

00:07:29.181 --> 00:07:30.516
-Mark Ronson, everybody!

00:07:30.516 --> 00:07:32.652
"Watch the Sound
with Mark Ronson"

00:07:32.652 --> 00:07:36.055
comes to Apple TV+ on July 30th.

